Syntax:   strcmp <opts> string1 op string2 <opts>Function: Carry out requested comparisonOptions:          -c	ignore case for comparison          -d	debug mode          -p	write reply to stdout          -s	suppress displaysOp codes:          eq    equal          lt    less than          gt    greater than          le    less or equal to          ge    greater or equal to          ne    not equal          ct    contains          bw    begins withResult: (returned as result code)          0     test was TRUE          1     test was FALSE          4     parameter errors foundThe case of option codes and operators is ignored.Strcmp was originally written to provide additional string comparison facilities for Sculptor programs, which at version 1:14.5 could not do caseless comparisons. It follows Unix shell conventions about TRUE/FALSEreplies and so can be used with any shell whose scripts provide conditionalprocessing. Strcmp has the sticky bit set so it can be used in loops withoutundue performance overheads.Typical use is:	if strcmp -c $name ct foo	then		echo $name contains Foo (found by a caseless comparison)	else		echo $name is foo-less	fiComparison strings should be enclosed in double quotes if they contain spacesand in single quotes if they contain words which the shell might interpret.The -d and -p options can be used to help debug your scripts. The -s optionis for use where the parameters may cause errors in strcmp that you want tohandle solely via the error reply. It is there to prevent error displays generated by strcmp from corrupting Sculptor forms displays:i1	input op	cmd = 'strcmp -cs "'+fname+'" '+op+' "'+tname+'"'	exec cmd	if tstat = 4 then\ 		error "Invalid comparison type supplied":\		goto i1	if tstat = 0 then message "Comparison succeeded"	if tstat = 1 then message "Comparison failed"Apologies to non-Sculptor freaks, but you should still get the gist of the above.The lt, gt, le and ge operators have their conventional argument orderings:"aaa lt aab" is TRUE. The ct and bw operators look for the second string asa substring within the first string. Strcmp always expects three arguments in the specified order. Extra arguments are ignored. As a result its error messages may sometimes be misleading if youdon't read them in this light, especially if the first string is unquoted and contains spaces.